course: myths
lesson: 25
title: Viral facts and chain messages
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Ping! "Send this to 10 people in 10 minutes or you'll have bad luck for 10 years!" Your thumb hovers over Forward, because what if?
explain: Chain messages and viral posts are built to make you feel something fast, fear, excitement or "OMG", so that you share before you think; that is how they spread like a cold.
explain: Some are harmless nonsense, some are scams that want your details or your parents' money, and a few viral facts are actually true, like giraffes having seven neck bones, the same as us.
explain: So pause before you press: who made this, what do they want, can I find it on a trusted news, fact-checking or official site, and what happens if I just don't share it?
explain: Understanding the trick protects you; you never need to make one. If a message scares you or asks for details, show a grown-up.
fact: Chain messages that threaten bad luck have no power; paper chain letters made the same threats long before phones existed.
fact: Posts offering free iPads or prizes for sharing and clicking are usually scams to collect personal details or spread harmful links; never click them, and tell an adult.
fact: Fake news of a celebrity's death spreads often; wait for trusted news sites or the person's official account before believing or sharing it.
fact: Typing a code you saw online into your phone will not give free credit; some codes can change your phone's settings, so never type codes from strangers.
fact: Messages saying WhatsApp will start charging unless you forward them have been shared for years and are hoaxes (tricks made up to fool people).
fact: Humans and giraffes really do have the same number of neck bones: seven, like most mammals; giraffes' are just much longer.
fact: A famous photo of a shark swimming on a flooded motorway is an edited fake that gets shared again after many big storms.
fact: Videos can be edited, staged or fooled by reflections, so a "100% real ghost" video is not evidence.
fact: No food or drink melts belly fat; lemon water is fine to drink, but it does not burn fat, and people who say a drink does may be selling something.
fact: Some scary warnings about viral "challenges" turn out to be hoaxes, and sharing them can spread the fear; check with a trusted source and tell an adult instead.
fact: You cannot charge a phone with an onion; such videos are staged (set up and acted out to look real).
fact: A viral post being shared millions of times does not make it true, and a few surprising viral facts are true, so check rather than sneer.
exercise: What happens if you don't forward a "bad luck for 10 years" message? | Nothing; chain messages have no power | You get bad luck for the next ten years | Your phone stops working by midnight tonight | The message sends itself to everyone anyway
exercise: Are chain letters new? | No, paper chain letters made the same threats long before phones | Yes, they were invented with WhatsApp | Yes, they started when phones got cameras | No, they were invented by the Romans for war
exercise: What do "free iPad for sharing" posts usually want? | Your personal details or to spread harmful links | To give free iPads to all the children who need them | To test how fast the internet is | To find out which school you go to for a prize
exercise: Where should you check news of a celebrity's death? | A trusted news site or their official account | The very first group chat that mentions it to you | A comment under a funny video with lots of likes | A message forwarded from someone you don't know
exercise: Why should you never type codes from strangers into your phone? | Some codes can change your phone's settings | Codes always delete all your photos | Typing codes uses up all your battery | Codes only work on your parents' phones, not yours
exercise: Is WhatsApp going to charge you unless you forward a message? | No, it is a hoax shared for years | Yes, unless you forward it ten times | Yes, but only children are charged | Only if you live in a big city
exercise: How many neck bones do giraffes have? | Seven, the same as humans | Seventy, ten times more than humans | Three huge ones | None; the neck is all muscle
exercise: What is the famous "shark on a motorway" photo? | An edited fake shared after big storms | A real photo taken during one bad flood | A photo of a toy shark floating in a bath | A photo of a dolphin that people called a shark
exercise: Why is a "100% real ghost video" not good evidence? | Videos can be edited, staged or fooled by reflections | Ghosts never show up on camera at all | Supermarkets don't allow filming | Only black-and-white videos are able to show real ghosts
exercise: Does lemon water melt belly fat? | No food or drink melts fat | Yes, it burns fat while you sleep | Yes, but only with warm water | Only if you drink it before breakfast
exercise: What should you do with a scary "dangerous challenge" warning? | Check with a trusted source and tell an adult | Share it to as many people as you can | Try the challenge yourself to see if it's real | Delete your account forever
exercise: Can you charge a phone with an onion? | No, those videos are staged | Yes, a red onion works best | Yes, but it takes a whole day | Only if you cut it in half first
exercise: Does sharing by millions make a post true? | No, popularity is not proof | Yes, so many people can't be wrong | Yes, if it has over a million likes | Only if a famous person shares it
exercise: What are viral messages built to do? | Make you feel something fast so you share before thinking | Teach you careful facts slowly | Make you put your phone down | Help you check the news calmly
exercise: What is the best first step before sharing a surprising post? | Pause and ask who made it and what they want | Share it fast before someone else does | Add more scary words to it | Send it to the youngest person you know, just in case
test: "SEND TO 10 PPL OR BAD LUCK 4 10 YEARS!!!" your little cousin is crying. What do you tell him? | It's just words on a screen; nothing will happen | Send it quickly, just in case | Send it to 20 to get double luck | Throw his phone in the bin to break the curse for good
test: Your group chat says a famous singer has died, but nothing is on the news. What's wise? | Wait for trusted news before believing or sharing | Post that she died, everyone else is | Message her family to ask | Delete the group chat
test: "omg free iPad just click n share!!!" your mate's already clicked. What should he do? | Tell an adult and don't enter any details | Share it with all his friends so they win too | Enter his address quickly before the offer ends | Click the link again to make sure it worked
test: A post says typing a code gets you free credit. What would really happen? | Nothing good; some codes change phone settings | You'd get £10 free credit | Your phone would get faster | Your battery would last forever
test: Your nan forwards "WhatsApp will start charging, pass it on!" What can you kindly tell her? | It's an old hoax; she doesn't need to forward it | It's true, she must forward it now | She'll be charged by WhatsApp unless she sends it to you | WhatsApp only charges grandparents
test: "giraffes have the same neck bones as us lol, fake" Who is right? | The viral fact: both have seven neck bones | Your mate: giraffes have far more | Neither: giraffes have none | Nobody knows because giraffes are too tall
test: After a storm, the shark-on-the-motorway photo is all over the class chat. How could you check it? | Search for it on a fact-checking site with an adult | Count how many people shared it | Zoom right in to see if the shark is smiling at the camera | Believe it because there was a storm
test: "lemon water melts belly fat, my fav influencer said so" What should you think? | No drink melts fat; the influencer may be selling something | It works, influencers always check facts | It works but only on Tuesdays | Drinking lemon water makes you thinner overnight
test: A scary "challenge" warning says share to warn parents. What could sharing do? | Spread fear about something that may be a hoax | Stop the challenge at once | Make the challenge illegal | Nothing, sharing has no effects ever
test: "this vid shows a phone charging off an onion, 2 mil views" What does 2 million views prove? | Nothing; popular videos can be staged | That onions really charge phones | That the video must be from a scientist | That only big onions work
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the efficacy of a message threatening misfortune upon a failure to forward it? | It possesses no capacity whatsoever to cause misfortune | It occasions misfortune solely upon its deletion | It occasions misfortune unless forwarded to ten recipients | Its efficacy is proportional to the number of recipients
exam: Identify the number of cervical vertebrae possessed by the giraffe. | Seven, being equal in number to those of humans | Seventy, being ten times the number found in humans | Three, each of exceptional length | None, the neck consisting solely of muscle
exam: It is given that an unsolicited communication offers an electronic device without charge in exchange for sharing and clicking a link. Determine the most probable purpose of such a communication. | The collection of personal details or the distribution of harmful links | The charitable distribution of devices to children | The measurement of internet connection speeds | The conduct of a lawful lottery
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the extent to which a claim is shared online. | The frequency of its sharing does not establish its truth | Its sharing by a million persons conclusively establishes its truth | Its sharing by a celebrity establishes its truth | A claim widely shared is invariably false
